        <div id="dwell-time" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Dwell Time: The Step Everyone Skips
          </h2>

          <p>
            RMR's direction for mold and mildew is to spray until the surface is thoroughly saturated, keep it wet for 10 minutes, then remove the excess liquid or let it air dry. Lowe's and other retailer listings repeat the same instruction, and one retailer summary adds the warning that removing it before 10 minutes means it will not kill the mold. Ten minutes sounds trivial. On a vertical surface in a warm basement, it is not.
          </p>

          <p>
            A fine mist on painted drywall or bare concrete can dry in two or three minutes, especially with a dehumidifier running. That is why owners who report that RMR-141 did nothing often turn out to have misted the area once and walked away. The fix is volume and a second pass. Use a pump sprayer with a coarse fan tip rather than a trigger bottle on anything larger than a shower, wet the surface until it glistens without running, and come back at the five-minute mark to re-wet anything that has dulled. Ceilings are the hardest: work small areas and accept that some will drip.
          </p>

          <p>
            Turn off fans and dehumidifiers that blow directly across the surface for those 10 minutes, then turn them back on. You want the surface wet for the contact time and then dry as quickly as possible afterwards, because drying is what actually stops regrowth. RMR's direction to let it air dry rather than rinse means some quat residue stays on the surface, which is consistent with the listing's claim that it inhibits odors and mildew when used as directed. Do not read that as permanent protection. Residual quat on a surface that gets wet again will not hold off mold for long.
          </p>

          <p>
            RMR-86 is the opposite. Its direction is to spray from 6 to 8 inches, wait at least 15 seconds, and rinse once the stain has gone. Leaving a bleach-based cleaner on wood or drywall for long periods does not improve the result and can damage the surface, and an unrinsed bleach residue is the last thing you want under a quat. If you are using both products, finish and rinse the stain remover, let the surface dry, and only then apply RMR-141 for its 10 minutes. Never let the two products mix on the surface or in a sprayer.
          </p>

