        <div id="porous-surfaces" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Porous vs Non-Porous Surfaces
          </h2>

          <p>
            Where RMR-141 works best and where it is close to pointless comes down to porosity. On a non-porous surface such as glazed tile, glass, sealed countertops, fiberglass or metal, mold sits on top, and a disinfectant that stays wet for its contact time reaches all of it. On those surfaces RMR-141 is doing exactly what its label describes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wood framing, plywood, OSB and bare concrete are semi-porous. Mold grows into the surface fibres and pores, not just on them. This is where RMR-141 earns its place in a cleanup. You cannot throw away a floor joist, and scrubbing removes surface growth but not what is in the grain. A thorough soak with a registered fungicide after scrubbing or sanding, followed by fast drying, is a reasonable way to deal with what you cannot physically remove. RMR markets RMR-141 for porous and non-porous surfaces, and wood is the case where that claim matters most. Read the label on your bottle for which claims apply to which surface types, because EPA labels often restrict mildew claims to hard, non-porous surfaces.
          </p>

          <p>
            Drywall, ceiling tiles, carpet, carpet pad, insulation and upholstery are the materials no spray can save. Drywall is gypsum wrapped in paper, and the paper is mold food. When mold is visible on the face, it is usually through the paper and often on the back, inside the wall cavity, where you cannot spray. The EPA's cleanup guidance says porous materials such as ceiling tiles and carpet may need to be thrown away if they become moldy, and that is the honest answer for drywall too. RMR-141 can kill what it touches on the surface. It cannot reach the back of the board, and dead mold left in place still causes allergic reactions.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical rule we use: if you can scrub it clean, scrub it and use RMR-141 as the final step. If you can sand it, sand it and treat it. If it is soft, absorbent, or the mold is coming through from behind, cut it out and bag it, then treat the framing behind it. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-get-rid-of-mold-in-basement/">getting rid of mold in a basement</a> walks through that sequence step by step, including how to tell mold from efflorescence on concrete before you spray anything.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-size" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Which Size and Kit to Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            RMR-141 is sold on Amazon in fewer configurations than you might expect. When we checked the listings, the standalone RMR-141 RTU appeared as a four-pack of 1-gallon bottles, while the smaller sizes came inside bundles: a gallon alongside a gallon of RMR-86, and a 32 oz bottle alongside RMR's Tub & Tile cleaner. Other retailers sell single 32 oz bottles and single gallons, so if you only need a little and want RMR-141 alone, it is worth checking a hardware store before buying a bundle you will not finish.
          </p>

          <p>
            Size the purchase by area and by how much product the 10-minute direction burns. A shower surround, bathroom ceiling or window frame is a 32 oz job, so the bathroom bundle fits. A laundry room, one basement wall or a deck is a gallon job, so the RMR-86 and RMR-141 bundle fits. A crawlspace, a basement with mold on joists and sill plates, or framing exposed after a leak is a multi-gallon job. Saturating rough lumber overhead uses liquid fast, and running out halfway through means part of the job gets a light coat that does not meet the contact time. The four-pack is the right buy there, and it is the listing our own readers buy most often.
          </p>

          <p>
            Stain remover needs are usually smaller. Most homes have a few square feet of visible staining even after a large mold problem, because the worst growth is on hidden framing nobody will see. A two-pack of 32 oz RMR-86 bottles covers most interior staining. The gallon of RMR-86 makes sense outside, on decks, siding and patios. RMR-86 Pro is for heavy staining on attic sheathing or crawlspace framing, typically before encapsulation or sealing, and is more than most homeowners need.
          </p>

